### According to material safety data sheets (MSDS) and toxicology studies, does PTFE at room temperature (i.e., not heated to decomposition) release significant amounts of toxic fumes or particles that could cause respiratory illness in humans?
Answer: According to the provided fluoride polymer handling guide, PTFE at room temperature does not release significant toxic fumes or particles. The document clearly states that hazardous decomposition products form only when fluoropolymers are heated above their recommended processing temperatures, with toxic fumes from PTFE beginning at 840°F (450°C). At room temperature, PTFE is described as a stable polymer with negligible residual monomers, and routine handling produces only non-hazardous nuisance dusts. Therefore, PTFE on masks at room temperature would not cause respiratory illness.

Source URL: https://www.turi.org/content/download/12048/189380/file/Guide%20to%20the%20Safe%20Handling%20of%20Fluoropolymer%20Resins%20v5%2020190130-1.pdf

